diff --git a/docs/platforms/mac/native-credentials.md b/docs/platforms/mac/native-credentials.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..a7a8dc2abfa926d6d863f5b84ae31df1a02d8a11
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/platforms/mac/native-credentials.md
@@ -0,0 +1,173 @@
+---
+summary: "Enter stored credentials into account-bound native macOS authentication prompts"
+read_when:
+  - Configuring autonomous Apple Account or Mac authentication
+  - Troubleshooting native credential prompt references
+  - Checking credential custody across paired Macs
+title: "Native credentials"
+---
+
+# Native credentials
+
+The macOS companion can resolve an enrolled credential in its local Keychain and
+enter it directly into a native secure password field. The agent passes an alias
+and an opaque prompt reference. Password values stay out of tool arguments,
+Gateway payloads, CUA input, clipboard operations, and trajectory text.
+
+This capability works with the companion's CUA and Peekaboo providers. The native
+host owns credential discovery and entry; ordinary provider window and element
+references cannot authorize a credential action.
+
+## Enroll an existing credential
+
+Passwords remain in existing generic-password items under Keychain service
+`com.openclaw.browser-credentials.v1`. Native enrollment adds non-secret authority
+to an existing item's metadata. It does not create or replace a password or change
+that item's access-control list.
+
+Run the Mac CLI on the Mac that will perform entry:
+
+```sh
+openclaw-mac credentials bind \
+  --alias apple.example.password \
+  --kind appleAccount \
+  --account operator@example.com \
+  --bundle com.apple.systempreferences
+
+openclaw-mac credentials bind \
+  --alias mac.example.password \
+  --kind macLogin \
+  --account 'Example User' \
+  --bundle com.apple.ScreenContinuity
+
+openclaw-mac credentials list
+```
+
+Use the identity shown in the actual authentication prompt and the alias for that
+account's existing credential. Enrollment records the current Mac's hardware
+identity automatically. Multiple `--bundle` options can authorize supported
+prompt owners for the same account. The CLI never accepts or prints a password.
+
+Supported native owners are:
+
+| Credential kind | Native owner bundle ID                  |
+| --------------- | --------------------------------------- |
+| `appleAccount`  | `com.apple.systempreferences`           |
+| `macLogin`      | `com.apple.LocalAuthentication.UIAgent` |
+| `macLogin`      | `com.apple.Passwords`                   |
+| `macLogin`      | `com.apple.ScreenContinuity`            |
+| `macLogin`      | `com.apple.SecurityAgent`               |
+
+Bundle matching also requires Apple's code-signing identity on the running
+process. Browser and web-view content use the separate browser credential route;
+an Apple-signed browser does not qualify as a native authentication prompt.
+
+Apple Passwords supports the English locked view shown after **Use Password**.
+Its lock heading, lock icon and account instruction must accompany a single
+secure field in the same native group. The displayed account must match the
+enrolled Mac login account in full. Add `com.apple.Passwords` explicitly to an
+existing binding while retaining its other intended owners; upgrading the app
+does not expand existing bindings. This unlocks the app only: it does not
+authorize entry into vault-item editors or provide a vault-item retrieval API.
+
+Enrollment belongs to each native host. A credential stored on one Mac is not
+implicitly transferred to another paired Mac. Configure the existing credential
+and its binding on the Mac that owns the target prompt; keep secret material out
+of model-visible transfer commands and artifacts.
+
+## Agent workflow
+
+For an authorized sign-in, use stored credentials proactively without asking the
+operator to re-enter a password that is already available through this route.
+
+1. Call `computer` with `action: "credential_prompts"`, optionally filtering by
+   `alias`, on the actual target Mac.
+2. Select the result matching the requested account and current prompt. Each
+   result contains the non-secret alias, account, kind, bundle ID and `promptRef`.
+3. Call `computer` with `action: "type_secret"`, that `alias`, and that exact
+   `promptRef` in the same execution.
+4. Observe the current prompt, submit through the appropriate ordinary control
+   if necessary, and verify the resulting account/service state.
+
+The computer tool supplies its execution ID. Direct operator invocations must
+also supply a current execution ID. Discovery replaces older prompt references
+for that execution. A reference is single-use and expires after 60 seconds.
+
+Entry reports `credentialEntry: "entered"` and
+`authenticationVerified: false`. It does not submit the dialog or establish that
+sign-in succeeded. A stale reference requires fresh discovery, not a blind retry.
+
+### Discovery diagnostics
+
+Discovery preserves the `credentialPrompts` rows and adds
+`credentialPromptDiagnostics` alongside them. Its closed `reason` values are:
+
+| Reason                       | Meaning                                                                                 |
+| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `focused_window_unavailable` | No focused native window was available to inspect.                                      |
+| `ax_metadata_unavailable`    | AX reads failed; missing metadata cannot establish that a password field is absent.     |
+| `secure_field_not_observed`  | The bounded snapshot did not identify a secure password field.                          |
+| `secure_field_ambiguous`     | More than one secure field was observed.                                                |
+| `form_context_missing`       | A secure field lacked a supported native authentication form context.                   |
+| `binding_absent`             | No binding matched the requested alias, or no bindings were available.                  |
+| `binding_unmatched`          | Existing bindings did not authorize the observed form.                                  |
+| `eligible`                   | At least one prompt reference passed the existing checks; authentication is not proven. |
+
+Counts report observed secure fields, AX read failures and examined prompt/binding
+pairs. `observedSecureFieldCount` is omitted when no snapshot was available.
+`bindingRejections` contains only counts keyed by `invalid_binding`,
+`host_mismatch`, `owner_not_bound` or `account_evidence_unmatched`.
+Expected unsupported or valueless optional AX attributes are not read failures.
+
+These diagnostics use the same discovery pass and contain no native text or
+credential values. Discovery reads binding metadata without resolving a secret;
+secure-field values are never read. Counts describe that bounded observation,
+not every window or authentication method. An empty result does not establish
+Touch ID use, a wrong password, or failed authentication. Existing authorization
+errors and entry checks remain unchanged.
+
+## Authority and failure handling
+
+The native host checks the current execution, credential binding, host, process,
+focused window, exact account evidence and secure field before resolving the
+credential and again immediately before entry. The field must remain enabled,
+settable, native and owned by the captured process. Password-change dialogs with
+multiple secure fields are outside this sign-in route. Secret field values are
+never read back for verification.
+
+Account evidence comes from the password field's native dialog or sheet.
+System Settings sidebar text cannot identify the account for another password
+form. Dedicated authentication owners can use their focused window as the form;
+System Settings requires a native dialog or sheet containing the account identity.
+Editable text-field contents do not supply account evidence. Discovery is bounded
+by both element count and time, and excludes web content.
+
+If no prompt matches, inspect the actual window and local binding metadata.
+Correct missing or stale configuration through the supported local owner. Do not
+resolve a password into ordinary `text`, `value`, shell, clipboard or recording
+inputs as a fallback.
+
+Keychain reads prohibit interactive authentication. An unavailable credential can
+therefore mean a missing item, a locked Keychain, or an item ACL that does not
+permit the signed companion to read it. Adding binding metadata alone does not
+prove credential readability or validity. Repair the specific local Keychain
+condition; preserve existing passwords and ACLs unless their change is separately
+part of the authorized repair.
+
+The existing desktop authority still applies. This route does not enter the Mac
+login window, unlock a secure screen, or bypass an OS-enforced biometric or human
+presence requirement. Such a requirement is distinct from an ordinary native
+password prompt supported above.
+
+## Verification
+
+Native acceptance requires all of the following on each intended Mac: the
+qualified companion build is running; its advertised computer capabilities
+include both actions; the correct local binding is readable; discovery identifies
+the actual prompt/account; entry reaches that secure field; and the service
+confirms the expected account after submission. Existing Keychain item metadata,
+a successful unit test, or entry into a field alone does not establish this.
+
+Use the [native test isolation guidance](/platforms/mac/dev-setup#run-native-tests-safely)
+for development. Pure credential-owner tests inject AX and Keychain operations;
+full native integration tests require the disposable macOS test environment.
